The accepted closure design follows the syntax migration. Compiler support is
pending. See @../progress (Compiler progress).

### Declaring and invoking a callback

A callback parameter or a callback field on a named struct declares a
signature. The closure supplied to that slot gets its argument types, access
modes and result from the signature. A trait requirement can declare a method
with a callback parameter under the same rules.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    row_label |title String, format_label |text String| -> String| -> String {
        return format_label(text = title)
    }

    prefix = "Action: "
    label = row_label("Save", |caption| => [prefix, caption])
]

Here `label` holds `"Action: Save"`. The *invoker* names the argument
`text` from the declaration. The *closure* calls the same argument
`caption` and captures `prefix` from the surrounding scope. Its argument
names obey the ordinary no-shadowing rule.

After `=>`, write one expression. Moth checks it as a return expression.
A callback can produce zero or one success value, including an optional value.
Omit the result arrow for zero values. Call a named helper when the work needs
statements. A closure literal requires an immediate receiving signature, even
if it captures nothing. You can't bind a bare literal to a local or return a
callback on its own. V1 provides no general function type, callable trait or
const closure.

### Capturing for the duration of a call

A plain callback parameter is valid for the receiving call. The receiver can
skip it, call it repeatedly or forward it to another plain parameter within
that call. Forming a closure identifies the outer places it uses. Invoking it
evaluates its body. Other arguments still evaluate in their written order.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    increment |value ~I32| {
        ~value += 1
    }

    twice |
        step ||,
    | {
        step()
        step()
    }

    count ~= 0
    twice(|| => increment(~count))
    io.line([:Count: [count]])
]

This prints `Count: 2`. Shared capture is the default. The `~count` argument
requests exclusive access for `increment`. Conflicts depend on the places
involved, including aliases. Passing another overlapping mutable argument to
the same receiving call would fail. A callback must leave its captures usable
for another invocation even if a particular receiver only calls it once.

To take one snapshot, write `saved = copy items` before forming a closure
that reads `saved`. Writing `copy items` inside the body takes a fresh copy on
each invocation. The same timing applies to a fallible lookup or any other
work you put in the body. See @../bindings/#explicit-copies (Explicit copies).

### Keeping a handler in a component

A callback parameter can grant permission to store or forward the callback
into a particular owner. Every path that keeps it must use the declared
destination.

- `into this` permits storage in the writable receiver.
- `into owner` permits storage in the writable parameter named `owner`.
- `into return` permits storage in the single success owner the function returns.

The owner may retain shared captures only when their storage and everything
they reference live long enough. Within that lifetime you can call a stored
field, replace it through writable access or lend it to a plain parameter.
Replacing a handler releases the old field's claim subject to outstanding
result borrows. Extracting the field doesn't grant permission to keep it in
another independent owner.

A retained handler can't capture an outside place exclusively. To edit
mutable state directly, lend it as an argument each time you call the handler.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Draft = | title String |
    Editor = | on_edit |model ~Draft, text String| |

    rename |document ~Draft, replacement String| {
        ~document.title = replacement
    }

    editor = Editor(|target, entered| => rename(~target, entered))
    current ~= Draft("Notes")
    editor.on_edit(~current, "Minutes")
    io.line(current.title)
]

The editor can live between edits. Only this invocation borrows `current`
exclusively. The caller can read `Minutes` as soon as the call completes.

### Copying a component or making its handler optional

A stored callback is non-copyable, even with no captures. If `panel.row.on_edit`
holds one, `copy panel` and `copy panel.row` both fail. You can copy an
eligible data field such as `copy panel.row.title`. An ordinary binding or a
legal return can still refer to the same owner.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    Suggestion = | suggest || -> String? |

    disabled Suggestion? = none
    installed Suggestion? = Suggestion(|| => "Untitled")
    no_suggestion = Suggestion(|| => none)
]

`disabled` has no handler. `installed` holds a handler that returns text.
`no_suggestion` also has a handler, but calling `no_suggestion.suggest()`
returns `none`. The `?` on `String?` belongs to the result. The field itself
requires a callback, so `Suggestion(none)` is invalid.

### Using a borrowed result

An invocation can return a view into an argument or a capture. Even a
zero-input reader such as `|| => task.title` can return borrowed text. When
only the signature is known, account for every possible source, including
references stored inside captured values. A drawing function can use the
text while those sources are live. Keeping the result longer requires proof
that each possible source outlives its destination, or an independent copy
made while the view is valid. Where applicable, a declared region can supply
the lifetime proof. See @../memory/#lifetimes-and-result-shapes (Lifetimes and result shapes).

### Failure and suspension

A callback runs synchronously. Its body, every reachable helper and any
implicit child join must have proof that they can't suspend. An imported
helper can qualify without a written `$no_suspend` when its effects prove
this. A channel wait or an unknown foreign effect prevents that proof. The
invoking function may wait between completed calls if its live borrows allow
it. See @../async/#channel-reference (Channels and tasks).

An invocation whose body is unknown may fail implicitly, trap or use fast
arithmetic. For example, checked arithmetic in the body can raise a built-in
failure. The code that invokes the callback owns that failure. Registration
can observe a failure when it invokes the handler synchronously. A later
invocation reports to its own caller. Storing the handler alone never executes
the body, and a failure after a committed store doesn't undo the store.

Private invokers can propagate implicit failure under the ordinary rules.
Exported invokers must recover or expose a compatible error boundary.
Callback signatures have no explicit error slot. Handle a named helper's
custom error inside the closure. See @../errors/#catch-and-recovery (Catch and recovery).
